Professional Conduct
Startups
Tines — Because Tines manages sensitive business automation workflows, placement students must strictly adhere to the company's internal data security procedures at all times. Misuse or accidental exposure of customer data could carry serious legal consequences under GDPR, so students are expected to handle all information with the utmost care and to flag any concerns immediately to their supervisor.
Solidroad — As Solidroad uses AI to process user data and simulate conversations, students must demonstrate a strong awareness of ethical responsibilities around AI transparency. Any personal data gathered during simulations must be handled in full compliance with GDPR, and students should avoid any action that could compromise user trust or data integrity.
SMEs
Workvivo — Workvivo processes large volumes of employee personal data on behalf of its clients, meaning placement students must uphold strict confidentiality at all times. Students are expected to comply with GDPR, avoid discussing or sharing internal client information outside of approved channels, and conduct themselves with the same professionalism expected of a permanent employee.
Phorest — Phorest manages sensitive customer and business data across thousands of salon clients, so students must abide by all relevant data protection legislation and any NDAs in place from the outset. No client information should be accessed or shared beyond what is strictly required for the student's role.
Multinationals
Google — Given that Google operates under stringent data privacy regulations across multiple jurisdictions and builds products used by billions of people, placement students are held to exceptionally high standards of ethical conduct. Students must follow all internal data handling protocols and understand the broader societal responsibility that comes with working at this scale.
Stripe — As a financial technology company subject to rigorous financial regulation and data protection law, Stripe requires that all staff — including placement students — handle payment data and user information with the highest levels of security and integrity. Any breach of conduct in this environment carries serious regulatory and reputational risk.

Communication Expectations
Startups
Tines — Working in a fast-moving startup environment, students at Tines are expected to communicate clearly and proactively across written channels such as Slack and email, as well as in team meetings. Because teams are small and cross-functional, being concise, responsive, and transparent about progress or blockers is essential to keeping workflows moving efficiently.
Solidroad — At Solidroad, students should be comfortable contributing ideas and feedback in an open, collaborative setting typical of early-stage companies. Communication is often informal and rapid, so students must be able to adapt their style accordingly — knowing when to ask questions, when to work independently, and how to surface issues without delay.
SMEs
Workvivo — Given that Workvivo's own product is built around internal communications and employee engagement, students are expected to model strong communication behaviours from day one. This means responding to messages in a timely manner, engaging constructively in team discussions, and using the company's own tools effectively.
Phorest — Students at Phorest should be able to communicate professionally across both technical and non-technical teams, adapting their language depending on the audience. Whether updating a manager on project progress or liaising with colleagues in product or support, clarity and reliability in communication are essential.
Multinationals
Google — Communication at Google is structured but high-expectation — students must be able to present ideas clearly, participate meaningfully in meetings, and produce well-written documentation. Given the scale and complexity of the organisation, students are also expected to communicate effectively across different teams and time zones where relevant.
Stripe — At Stripe, written communication is particularly valued, as much of the company's internal culture is built around clear, thoughtful documentation. Students should be comfortable drafting detailed written updates, asking precise questions, and contributing to async communication channels without relying on constant supervision.
